it's the biggest diplomatic gathering in  the world with delegations from all the  countries that the members of the United  Nations in two and a half years heading  the UN secretary-general Antonio  Guterres has made great efforts to  resolve the world's most deadly  conflicts but with little progress in an  interview for Al Jazeera's planet SOS he  told me he wants the focus of this  year's high-level week on an issue he  says is even more important climate the  threat of each of these conflicts is to  a certain extent localized and climate  change became a global threat for  humankind and the global set for the  planet and things are evolving in such a  way that climate change became an  accelerated of conflict with tension  growing between the u.s. Saudi Arabia  and Iran the situation in the Gulf will  be high on the agenda and one of the  UN's most experienced diplomats told me  with so many key players in the same  place at the same time there could be  important developments things can move  very fast and it would be foolish to  rule something out on grounds of  ideology when as you say you've got all  these world leaders and foreign  ministers together it does mean that  reactions can be very Swift  and initiatives can be taken privately  without having to have the full  publicity of a leader fly in or go  somewhere every year the General  Assembly week gets busier and busier  today the count is 90 once heads of  state six vice presidents 45 heads of  government five deputy Prime Minister's  the number of meetings requested has now  climbed to 630 this part of the UN the  visitors Lobby is normally open to the  public but during the high-level week  it's one of the most secure places on  earth all around the UN temporary rooms  have been set up these are what are  known as bilateral booths you can see  the leather chairs the table there's  room here for two or more delegations to  meet at short notice if necessary in  some privacy  organizing this event is a mammoth  operation insiders have told me just  getting the motorcades of presidents  prime ministers and foreign ministers  here so they can speak back to back on  the podium is an operation akin to air  traffic control James BAE's al-jazeera  of the United Nations
 

